WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. This class turns AI-assisted
research into a bounded, reviewable

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
evidence workflow. You will begin with the
decision the research

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
must support, not with an open-ended
request for facts. You

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
will build a source ledger, separate
generated leads from opened

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
sources, verify every material claim,
preserve disagreement, and recover when

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
evidence fails. The goal is not a polished
answer at

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
any cost. The goal is a synthesis another
person can

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
reproduce, challenge, and safely use.

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Prepare before searching. State the
decision this work supports and

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
the people who will use it. Break the need

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
into bounded questions that can be
answered with observable evidence.

12
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Define scope such as date range, region,
product version, audience,

13
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
and excluded topics. Then state what
evidence is strong enough

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
for each kind of claim. Current product
behavior may require

15
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.000
official documentation or a repeatable
test. A comparison may require

16
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
equivalent measurements. A forecast should
expose assumptions rather than masquerade

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
as a fact. Record which data is permitted
and which

18
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
must stay out of the tool. Finally, define
stop conditions.

19
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Stop when required claims have qualified
support, when time expires,

20
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
when sources conflict beyond the
workflow's authority, or when the

21
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
question must be narrowed. A brief
prevents the model from

22
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
silently expanding the task and gives the
reviewer a standard

23
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.000
for deciding whether the research is
complete.

24
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Suppose a team asks, which model should we
adopt? That

25
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
question is too broad. I rewrite the
decision as: choose

26
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
a model for summarizing approved support
articles in English during

27
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.000
a thirty-day pilot. I ask about answer
quality on a

28
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
named test set, latency under a stated
load, handling of

29
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
unsupported claims, data-use constraints,
and estimated cost for the pilot

30
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
volume. I limit the scope to currently
available versions and

31
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
the deployment regions the organization
can use. I require provider

32
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
documentation for service behavior,
measured tests for quality and latency,

33
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
and reviewed pricing evidence for
estimates. Confidential tickets are

34
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
excluded; the pilot uses approved
synthetic or redacted material. We

35
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
stop when every required field has
evidence, a visible gap,

36
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
or an accountable escalation. The narrower
brief does not guarantee

37
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
the right choice, but it makes the
research testable.

38
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.667
Execute with a source ledger. For each
candidate source, record

39
00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:31.333
title, publisher, stable location,
publication or review date, the exact

40
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:37.000
claims it may support, its scope and
limitations, and your

41
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.667
access date. Prefer primary sources for
product behavior, standards, original

42
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:48.333
research, and official policy. Use
secondary sources to discover leads

43
00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:54.000
or understand context, then verify
consequential claims against stronger

44
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.667
evidence. AI can suggest search terms,
extract candidate statements, group

45
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:05.333
themes, and point out missing questions.
Those outputs are research

46
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:11.000
assistance, not sources. Open the original
material and confirm that

47
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.667
it exists, is current enough, and actually
says what the

48
00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:22.333
draft claims. Search snippets and
citation-shaped strings are especially

49
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:28.000
easy to misread because they remove
surrounding scope. Preserve rejected

50
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.667
sources and reasons when that decision
affects the conclusion. A

51
00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:39.333
ledger makes selection visible and
prevents the final prose from

52
00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:45.000
becoming the only record of how evidence
was chosen.

53
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Choose one current, low-risk question. Add
one source-ledger row. Record

54
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
the publisher, access date, one exact
claim the source can

55
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
support, one limitation, and a status of
lead, verified, disputed,

56
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
or unsupported. If you have not opened the
source, it

57
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.000
must remain a lead.

58
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Verify material claims one by one. Copy
the claim into

59
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.000
a claim map, identify the source, and
point to the

60
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.000
passage, table, test, or record that
supports it. Check whether

61
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:04.000
the source covers the same version,
region, population, measurement, and

62
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.000
time period as the claim. Separate a
direct statement from

63
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.000
your inference. A source can be
authoritative and still fail

64
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.000
to support a broader sentence. A citation
can resolve and

65
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.000
still point to irrelevant text. When two
qualified sources disagree,

66
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.000
preserve both positions, dates, and
scopes. Do not average incompatible

67
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:40.000
claims or select the wording that sounds
most confident. Seek

68
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.000
an updated authority, a reproducible test,
or accountable review. Scale

69
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:52.000
corroboration to consequence. A low-risk
orientation may need careful comparison;

70
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.000
a security, health, access, or financial
decision may need independent

71
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.000
evidence and a named reviewer. Make
unsupported by the available

72
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:10.000
evidence an acceptable result.

73
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Checkpoint. An official page confirms that
a feature exists. A

74
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.000
separate table lists availability in three
regions. The draft says

75
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.000
the feature is available in every region.
How should the

76
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.000
claim map classify that sentence, and what
should the synthesis

77
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.000
say?

78
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.000
The evidence proves that the feature
exists and names three

79
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.000
available regions. It does not prove
availability everywhere. Classify the

80
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.000
global statement as unsupported. A
defensible synthesis says the supplied

81
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.000
evidence supports the three listed regions
and does not establish

82
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.000
global availability. Record the missing
current region authority as a

83
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
gap. If you chose the official feature
page as proof

84
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.000
of every region, revise the scope check:
authority for existence

85
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.000
is not evidence for geographic coverage.
If you removed the

86
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.000
claim without recording the gap, add the
gap so the

87
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.000
next researcher knows what evidence is
still needed.

88
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.333
Evidence failure is a normal research
outcome, so design recovery

89
00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:30.667
before it happens. If a link is
inaccessible, look for

90
00:10:30.667 --> 00:10:36.000
the canonical publisher, an archived
version permitted by policy, or

91
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.333
another primary record. If evidence is
stale, narrow the claim

92
00:10:41.333 --> 00:10:46.667
to the supported date and search for a
current source.

93
00:10:46.667 --> 00:10:52.000
If several articles cite one unsupported
statement, mark the chain

94
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.333
as circular rather than counting it as
independent agreement. If

95
00:10:57.333 --> 00:11:02.667
the source is ambiguous, quote the exact
limitation and ask

96
00:11:02.667 --> 00:11:08.000
a narrower question. If the research
cannot support the requested

97
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.333
scope, produce a gap report instead of a
plausible completion.

98
00:11:13.333 --> 00:11:18.667
State what is supported, what is disputed,
what is missing,

99
00:11:18.667 --> 00:11:24.000
what searches or tests were attempted, and
the next safe

100
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.333
action. Recovery can also mean stopping:
time, cost, access, or

101
00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:34.667
authority limits may require escalation. A
visible gap is useful

102
00:11:34.667 --> 00:11:40.000
evidence about the decision's uncertainty.

103
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.000
Test recovery by removing a source.
Imagine a synthesis with

104
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:50.000
three claims. Official documentation
supports current capability. A measured

105
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.000
test supports latency under one load. A
reviewed price sheet

106
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:00.000
supports a thirty-day estimate. I remove
the test. The capability

107
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.000
and cost claims may remain, but the
latency conclusion no

108
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:10.000
longer has evidence. The correct response
is not to let

109
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.000
the model reconstruct a likely number. I
mark the latency

110
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
claim unsupported, name the missing test
conditions, and choose whether

111
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.000
to rerun the test, find an equivalent
independent measurement, or

112
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.000
omit latency from the decision. This
source-removal test reveals hidden

113
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.000
dependence and confirms that the claim map
changes when its

114
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:40.000
evidence changes.

115
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:46.154
Protect data and preserve provenance
throughout the workflow. Use the

116
00:12:46.154 --> 00:12:52.308
minimum material necessary. Remove
identifiers that do not affect the

117
00:12:52.308 --> 00:12:58.462
question. Do not upload confidential,
personal, licensed, or restricted content

118
00:12:58.462 --> 00:13:04.615
unless policy, authorization, service
terms, and retention controls permit it.

119
00:13:04.615 --> 00:13:10.769
Treat instructions inside retrieved pages
as untrusted content, not permission

120
00:13:10.769 --> 00:13:16.923
to change the task or use tools. Preserve
publisher, title,

121
00:13:16.923 --> 00:13:23.077
location, access date, supported claims,
and limitations separately from generated

122
00:13:23.077 --> 00:13:29.231
prose. Respect attribution and license
requirements when quoting or adapting

123
00:13:29.231 --> 00:13:35.385
material. Before sharing, open every
material citation, check that sensitive

124
00:13:35.385 --> 00:13:41.538
data is absent, verify that uncertainty
remains visible, and confirm

125
00:13:41.538 --> 00:13:47.692
that the conclusion does not exceed the
brief. A trustworthy

126
00:13:47.692 --> 00:13:53.846
synthesis is reproducible from its ledger
without relying on hidden

127
00:13:53.846 --> 00:14:00.000
reasoning or the model's confidence.

128
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.625
Open the evidence-led research activity.
Choose a current, low-risk question.

129
00:14:05.625 --> 00:14:11.250
Complete the decision, questions, scope,
evidence, allowed-data, and stop-condition

130
00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:16.875
fields. Build at least three qualified
source rows. Draft a

131
00:14:16.875 --> 00:14:22.500
one-page synthesis that maps each material
claim to evidence and

132
00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:28.125
labels disagreement or uncertainty. Then
remove one source, identify exactly

133
00:14:28.125 --> 00:14:33.750
what no longer holds, and write the
recovery action. Save

134
00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:39.375
the brief, ledger, synthesis, claim map,
and gap note as

135
00:14:39.375 --> 00:14:45.000
your evidence.

136
00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.571
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

137
00:17:48.571 --> 00:17:52.143
identify a complete research brief,
classify an AI-generated source

138
00:17:52.143 --> 00:17:55.714
suggestion, preserve disagreement between
authoritative sources, respond when

139
00:17:55.714 --> 00:17:59.286
evidence cannot support the requested
scope, and recognize what makes

140
00:17:59.286 --> 00:18:02.857
a synthesis reproducible. Review the class
or return to your

141
00:18:02.857 --> 00:18:06.429
activity before submitting. The assessment
begins only when you choose

142
00:18:06.429 --> 00:18:10.000
Begin knowledge check.

143
00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:15.000
Remember the boundary: AI can assist
research, but it is

144
00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:20.000
not the evidence. Bound the question,
qualify sources, map claims,

145
00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:25.000
preserve disagreement, and make gaps
useful.
